<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!-- These two are the only permissions this project declares. Note what
         is absent: no internet access, and no all-package-visibility
         permission. The allow-list resolves labels and icons through the
         explicit <queries> list below instead (spec 9.6). Neither absent
         permission is named here in full, so grepping this tree for either
         string stays a meaningful check.

         The MERGED manifest is no longer exactly these two, and saying so
         here is cheaper than discovering it during a Play Store review.
         androidx.work's own AAR manifest declares WAKE_LOCK,
         ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, FOREGROUND_SERVICE and RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED,
         and androidx.core adds its signature level
         DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION, so a build carrying the
         stage two parse worker ships six. None of them is network access or
         package visibility, and spec 11.4's Data Safety claim of "no data
         collected or shared" is unaffected, but the set is not two and
         nothing in this file could have told you that. The components
         WorkManager merges in are its own. The two that are exported
         (SystemJobService, DiagnosticsReceiver) are guarded by
         BIND_JOB_SERVICE and DUMP respectively, both of which only the system
         and the shell hold. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <queries>
        <package android:name="my.com.tngdigital.ewallet" />
        <package android:name="com.maybank2u.life" />
    </queries>

    <!--
         On tools:ignore below. Lint asks for fullBackupContent alongside
         dataExtractionRules because minSdk is 27 and the latter only applies
         from 31. It is wrong for this configuration, and adding a
         fullBackupContent file to satisfy it would be dead weight implying a
         backup this app never permits:

           API 27 to 30   allowBackup="false" disables backup outright. There
                          is nothing for fullBackupContent to scope.
           API 31+        allowBackup="false" stops cloud backup but NOT
                          device to device transfer, which is exactly what the
                          device-transfer block in data_extraction_rules.xml
                          is there to exclude (spec 11.1).

         Suppressed rather than left standing, because a warning known to be
         noise is a warning that hides the next real one.
    -->
    <application
        android:name="my.pinged.PingedApp"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="Pinged"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Pinged"
        tools:ignore="DataExtractionRules">

        <!--
            WorkManager on demand, rather than at every process start.

            androidx.startup's WorkManagerInitializer builds WorkManagerImpl,
            opens WorkManager's own Room database and posts ForceStopRunnable
            during Application.onCreate. This app's processes are created
            constantly and for one reason: the system binding the listener to
            deliver a notification, most of which are discarded at the
            allow-list gate without any work ever being enqueued.

            Removing the initializer and implementing Configuration.Provider
            moves that to the first WorkManager.getInstance call, which is
            ParseWorker.enqueue, i.e. only once a row has actually been
            written, or on the rebind and foreground paths that enqueue
            anyway. See PingedApp.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.startup.InitializationProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.androidx-startup"
            android:exported="false"
            tools:node="merge">
            <meta-data
                android:name="androidx.work.WorkManagerInitializer"
                tools:node="remove" />
        </provider>

        <!-- The only Activity, and the only exported component this project
             declares. Exported because a launcher entry requires it: without
             android:exported="true" alongside the LAUNCHER category the app
             has no icon and cannot be started at all, and on API 31+ the
             build fails outright rather than installing something unlaunchable.

             No android:launchMode. The default (standard) is what a single
             launcher activity wants; singleTask here would silently change
             how a future deep link into a transaction behaves.

             windowSoftInputMode is deliberately absent, because nothing on
             this screen takes text. -->
        <activity
            android:name="my.pinged.MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Pinged">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!-- Stage one. The class name is frozen: the notification-access
             grant is stored against this flattened ComponentName, so renaming
             or moving the class voids the grant silently, with no callback
             and nothing the user can see (spec 10.1). -->
        <service
            android:name="my.pinged.capture.PingedNotificationListener"
            android:exported="false"
            android:label="Pinged"
            android:permission="android.permission.BIND_NOTIFICATION_LISTENER_SERVICE">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.service.notification.NotificationListenerService" />
            </intent-filter>
        </service>

        <!-- The first of spec 10.1's three rebind paths. Replacing the APK
             unbinds the listener; where nothing rebinds it, capture dies on
             every build installed and the symptom looks like a parser that
             stopped matching rather than a binding that went away.

             Measured on this project's emulator (AOSP API 37, 2026-09-05):
             the platform DOES rebind on its own after a package update.
             NotificationManagerService rebound the listener 143ms before this
             receiver ran, and the receiver's requestRebind was answered with
             "is already bound". Spec 10.1 asserts flatly that nothing rebinds
             until a reboot or a permission toggle, and on current AOSP that is
             no longer true. The receiver stays because the OEM ROMs the whole
             reliability section exists for are exactly the ones that do not do
             this, because the claim held on the platform versions this app
             supports down to minSdk 27, and because when it is redundant it
             costs one no-op call. What it can no longer be called is the thing
             standing between a reinstall and silent capture death on every
             device.

             exported="false" is correct even though the sender is the system.
             Both actions are protected broadcasts that only the system may
             send, and the system is exempt from the exported check.
             androidx.work's own RescheduleReceiver, which this manifest merges,
             is declared the same way for BOOT_COMPLETED.

             MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED needs no <data> element; PACKAGE_REPLACED, the
             one that reports about *other* apps, is the one that does. Both
             actions are also exempt from the API 26 implicit-broadcast ban.

             Not directBootAware: BOOT_COMPLETED arrives after the first unlock,
             which is what is wanted here, because before it neither the
             Keystore key nor credential-encrypted storage exists (spec 10.5)
             and a listener bound then could not open the database. -->
        <receiver
            android:name="my.pinged.capture.RebindReceiver"
            android:exported="false">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MY_PACKAGE_REPLACED" />
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
            </intent-filter>
        </receiver>
    </application>

</manifest>
